WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.207
A two-dimensional NumPy array can be thought of as a grid of rows and columns.

00:00:06.207 --> 00:00:08.652
In this example, each row contains three scores.

00:00:08.652 --> 00:00:12.802
Click Run. Shape tells us the size of the matrix.

00:00:12.802 --> 00:00:17.797
The expression scores zero comma colon selects the entire first row.

00:00:17.797 --> 00:00:22.572
Scores colon comma one selects the entire second column.

00:00:22.572 --> 00:00:26.912
Scores two comma one selects one specific value: the element in the third row and second column.

00:00:26.912 --> 00:00:34.797
This row-and-column notation is fundamental when working with numerical datasets.

00:00:34.797 --> 00:00:38.722
In analytics, rows often represent observations and columns often represent variables,

00:00:38.722 --> 00:00:41.177
although the meaning depends on the project.

00:00:41.177 --> 00:00:46.887
For example, rows could represent patients and columns could represent measurements.

00:00:46.887 --> 00:00:53.632
Or rows could represent products and columns could represent months.

00:00:53.632 --> 00:00:58.182
Always connect the array dimensions to the real-world meaning of the data.

00:00:58.182 --> 00:01:03.960
A numerical matrix is much easier to understand when you know what each axis represents.
